dict(n=12, orig=22, answer='B', type='Parallel reasoning',
     passage=[('prem', 'Criminals often give themselves away by their lifestyle.'),
              ('conc', 'My neighbour is a criminal'),
              ('evid', 'because he bought and is living in a very expensive house,'),
              ('prem', 'which I know he could not afford as he is officially working only in a poorly paid job.')],
     stem='Which one of the following most clearly parallels the reasoning used in the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'The cat keeps bringing mice into the house. He never eats them, so I assume he hunts them either because that is his instinct or for pleasure.',
              'B': 'It is impossible to write more than four pages in half an hour. You have just handed in seven pages of writing. You must, therefore, have spent longer than half an hour on it.',
              'C': 'The bank was extremely well guarded and robbing it would require very careful planning. The thieves who broke in yesterday must have spent months planning every detail of the crime.',
              'D': 'All my friends save money through the year and then go on holiday. I am saving every month, so I think I should be able to go on holiday soon.',
              'E': 'If the police want to identify criminals they need to watch how they are spending their money. They do want to catch them, so they watch out for people buying cars or houses they cannot legally afford.'},
     right='Same shape: the result is more than the stated limit allows, so something beyond the official story must be going on.',
     why={'A': 'Rules out one motive (food) and lists the rest. No result that breaks a limit.',
          'C': '“B needs A; B happened; so A”. No official story is contradicted (and “months” is added).',
          'D': 'Copies what friends do to predict a holiday. A comparison, not a contradiction.',
          'E': 'Same <b>topic</b> (criminals, houses they cannot afford), but it is “if they want X they must do Y”. Different shape.'},
     working_title='Working: strip it to a skeleton',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Original: official limit {ar('pen')} he can only afford what a <b>poorly paid job</b> allows. Result {ar('pen')} he has a <b>very expensive house</b>, beyond that limit. So {ar('pen')} he must have <b>something hidden</b> (crime).</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>B</b><span>Limit: at most four pages in half an hour. Result: seven pages. So you must have had something extra (more time). {tick()} Same shape.</span></div>
<div><b>A</b><span>Not for food, so instinct or pleasure. Eliminating a motive, no limit broken. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>C</b><span>Robbery needs planning; it happened; so they planned. Nothing they were known to have is exceeded. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>Friends save then holiday; I save; so I will holiday. Copying a pattern. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>E</b><span>If they want X, they must do Y; they want X; so they do Y. Topic match only. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('Ignore the crime topic (it is the trap in E). Look for “the result is more than the known means allow, so there must be a hidden extra”.', 'tip')}"""),

dict(n=13, orig=23, answer='C', type='Principle',
     passage=[('ctx', 'There is often concern about the large quantities of money earned by top singers.'),
              ('counter', 'It could be argued that there are many ways in which this money could be spent that would be beneficial to a large number of people.'),
              ('conc', 'However, these people are receiving high earnings because they are the best at what they do'),
              ('prem', 'and those who are better than others at what they do should be rewarded.')],
     stem='Which one of the following best illustrates the principle underlying the argument above?',
     options={'A': 'To become a doctor requires a much longer course of study than other professions; therefore, doctors should receive a higher rate of pay than members of those other professions.',
              'B': 'When making the decision about who should be accepted onto the course, the bottom third of the applicants based on the results were eliminated immediately.',
              'C': 'Since efficiency is the most important aspect of the work, the end of year bonus was awarded to the member of staff who had made the best use of time over the year.',
              'D': 'When deciding on the member of staff to be promoted, the director of the company looked at the records of the applicants and chose the one who had done the most overtime in the past year.',
              'E': 'The prize for the best film was decided by taking the one that made the most money at the box office in its opening week.'},
     right='Principle: whoever is <b>best at what they do</b> should be rewarded. The bonus goes to the person who was best at the thing that matters most (efficiency).',
     why={'A': 'Rewards a longer training, not being better than others.',
          'B': 'Removes the worst. It is a selection, not a reward for the best.',
          'D': 'Rewards the most hours worked, which is not the same as doing the job best.',
          'E': 'Uses money to decide what is “best”: the passage goes the other way (being best earns the money).'},
     working_title='Working: write the principle first',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Singers earn a lot {ar('pen')} because they are the <b>best</b> at what they do {ar('pen')} and the best <b>should be rewarded</b>.<br>
So the principle: <b>reward people for being better than others at what they do</b>.</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>C</b><span>Efficiency is what counts → bonus to the most efficient person. Best performer rewarded. {tick()}</span></div>
<div><b>A</b><span>Pay for a longer course. Rewards the training, not being better. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>B</b><span>Cut the bottom third. Punishes the worst; no reward. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>Most overtime. Rewards quantity of hours, not quality. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>E</b><span>Most money = best film. Earnings decide “best”, the wrong way round. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('Check what is actually being rewarded in each option: skill, effort, hours, money or luck.', 'tip')}"""),

