dict(n=15, orig=28, answer='E', type='Parallel reasoning',
     passage=[('conc', 'It is clear that telepathy is not possible'),
              ('prem', 'because, if it were, people would be able to transfer their thoughts to each other for important purposes – for example, passing an examination.'),
              ('evid', 'But this does not happen. The only supposed demonstrations of telepathy are about trivial matters.')],
     stem='Which one of the following arguments has the same logical structure as the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'It is obvious that humans cannot travel through time. No one has ever done this, except in novels and films, and we know that these are fiction.',
              'B': 'If humans were descended from apes, they would have some of the same patterns of behaviour. There are some patterns of behaviour common to humans and apes. So humans are descended from apes.',
              'C': 'If hypnotism could cure illnesses, we would not have to rely on drugs. But hypnotism cannot cure illness. So we do have to rely on drugs.',
              'D': 'It is evident that human life is the most intelligent form of life on Earth, because humans can exploit other life forms for their own purposes, and that is exactly what the most intelligent life form would have to be able to do.',
              'E': 'There is no evidence that intelligent life exists beyond our planet. If there were intelligent life elsewhere in the universe, we would by now have had clear evidence of its existence. So there is no intelligent life other than that on Earth.'},
     right='Same structure: if A, then we would see B; we do not see B; so not A.',
     why={'A': 'No “if… then” at all: it just says it has never been done.',
          'B': '“If A then B; B; so A” runs the rule backwards.',
          'C': '“If A then B; not A; so not B” denies the start, not the result.',
          'D': '“A must be able to do B; humans do B; so humans are A”. Backwards again.'},
     working_title='Working: strip it to a skeleton',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Original: if <b>A</b> (telepathy possible) {ar('pen')} then <b>B</b> (used for important things like exams). <b>Not B</b> (it does not happen) {ar('pen')} so <b>not A</b>.</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>E</b><span>If A (intelligent life elsewhere) → B (clear evidence by now). Not B (no evidence) → so not A. {tick()} Same shape (the order of the sentences is shuffled).</span></div>
<div><b>A</b><span>Never done → impossible. No conditional rule. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>B</b><span>If A → B. B → so A. Affirms the result. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>C</b><span>If A → B. Not A → so not B. Denies the start. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>The most intelligent must be able to exploit; humans exploit → so humans are the most intelligent. Backwards. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('Mark each sentence as “if A then B”, “A”, “not A”, “B” or “not B”, whatever order they come in.', 'tip')}"""),

dict(n=16, orig=29, answer='A', type='Assumption',
     passage=[('ctx', 'For the second year running the Silver Star Prize for art was awarded to a video artist, raising again the big question: What is great art?'),
              ('counter', "Many have condemned this year's choice on the grounds that a documentary-style video film cannot be considered as creative art at all in the way that, say, painting and sculpture can."),
              ('evid', "The Silver Star jury, however, praised the 'emotional force of the work and its complexity beneath an apparently simple surface'."),
              ('conc', 'If they are right in this evaluation then clearly video is as much a medium for great art as any other form of expression.')],
     stem='Which one of the following is an underlying assumption of the argument above?',
     options={'A': 'Any work with emotional force and complexity is capable of being great art.',
              'B': 'The decision of the jury to award the prize for a video was the right one.',
              'C': 'No-one can really answer the question: What is great art?',
              'D': "This year's winning exhibit was deceptively simple.",
              'E': 'Painting and sculpture are the highest forms of creative art.'},
     right='“Emotional force and complexity, so video can be great art” only works if emotional force and complexity are enough to make great art possible.',
     why={'B': 'The argument says “<b>if</b> they are right”, so it does not assume the jury was right.',
          'C': 'The argument gives an answer (emotional force and complexity), so it does not assume there is none.',
          'D': 'Again covered by “if they are right”. It is the jury’s view, not assumed.',
          'E': 'This sides with the critics and would weaken the argument.'},
     gap='When a conclusion starts “If they are right…”, the jury’s view is not assumed. Look for the link from their words to “great art”.'),

dict(n=17, orig=33, answer='C', type='Weaken',
     passage=[('evid', "'Organic food is no healthier than other produce, scientists claim.' This was the headline in an article published in 2009 in <i>The Independent</i>. It reported that a comprehensive review of 50 years of evidence showed no appreciable difference in nutrient levels between organic and conventional farm produce."),
              ('ic', 'If the review is correct shoppers will think twice about buying organic food, especially if the price difference remains significant.'),
              ('conc', 'Only if people really do receive some health benefit in return for the extra money will the current upward trend in organic sales continue.')],
     stem='Which one of the following presents the strongest challenge to the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'Shoppers who read <i>The Independent</i> are more likely to buy organic produce than readers of other newspapers.',
              'B': 'Most shoppers take more notice of price than they do of nutritional benefits when choosing to buy.',
              'C': 'If the review is correct, shoppers will have received no health benefit in the past 50 years, yet organic sales have continued to rise.',
              'D': 'If organic food had more nutritional value than conventional farm produce, scientists would have found out long before now.',
              'E': 'If there is no appreciable difference in nutrient levels there is no advantage for shoppers in switching back to conventional produce.'},
     right='Sales have risen for 50 years with no health benefit. So rising sales do <b>not</b> depend on a health benefit, and the “only if” claim fails.',
     why={'A': 'If organic buyers read the headline, they are more likely to think twice: this fits the argument.',
          'B': 'Price-conscious shoppers would think twice about dearer organic food: this supports it.',
          'D': 'Backs up the review’s finding: this supports it.',
          'E': 'Ignores price (conventional food is cheaper) and does not touch the “only if” claim.'},
     gap='An “only if” claim is beaten by one case where the result happened without the condition.'),

dict(n=18, orig=34, answer='B', type='Flaw',
     passage=[('ctx', 'There is widespread dissatisfaction and disillusionment in Britain with politics and with politicians, who are seen as untrustworthy. Many of the electorate do not even bother to vote in a general election.'),
              ('ctx', 'In Switzerland democracy is more direct than in Britain, in the sense that many referendums take place, and many important political decisions are thus based directly on the votes of citizens rather than on those of their elected representatives.'),
              ('evid', 'Surveys show that in those parts of Switzerland where more of these important decisions are made by referendum, the citizens are happier than in those areas where there is less opportunity to influence decisions.'),
              ('conc', 'So if we were to adopt the Swiss system in Britain, the dissatisfied and disillusioned British electorate would be much happier.')],
     stem='Which one of the following identifies the flaw in the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'It assumes that most Swiss citizens vote in general elections.',
              'B': 'It assumes that there is only one reason why some Swiss citizens are happier.',
              'C': 'It assumes that British citizens would trust politicians if there were more referendums.',
              'D': 'It assumes that there is only one reason why the British are disillusioned with politics.',
              'E': 'It assumes that better policies emerge when important decisions are made by referendum.'},
     right='More referendums and more happiness go together, but other things could make those Swiss areas happier. It treats the link as the cause.',
     why={'A': 'Swiss general election turnout is never part of the reasoning.',
          'C': 'The conclusion is about being happier, not about trusting politicians.',
          'D': 'Tempting, but the argument never names a cause of British disillusion. The broken step is reading cause into the Swiss survey.',
          'E': 'Better policies are never mentioned; the claim is only about happiness.'},
     gap='Two things found together (referendums, happiness) does not mean one causes the other.'),

dict(n=19, orig=35, answer='B', type='Main conclusion',
     passage=[('counter', "Sound economics may underlie the Government's plans to put more prisons under private control,"),
              ('conc', 'but the privatisation of prisons is nonetheless wrong.'),
              ('prem', 'Although some countries observe basic human rights, they have the power to take away the liberty of individuals who break certain laws. This power is so far-reaching that only the State itself, acting through its officers, should be entrusted with it.'),
              ('prem', 'Many prisoners have disturbed backgrounds and a contempt for authority, so exceptional disciplinary measures are needed to maintain order.'),
              ('ic', 'The power to enforce such measures should not be left to private, profit-making organisations.')],
     stem='Which of the following best expresses the main conclusion of the argument above?',
     options={'A': 'The State has far-reaching powers and duties.',
              'B': 'The privatisation of prisons is wrong.',
              'C': 'Order in prisons often requires special disciplinary measures.',
              'D': 'Prison inmates often have disturbed backgrounds and a contempt for authority.',
              'E': 'Special disciplinary powers should not be entrusted to private organisations.'},
     right='“…but the privatisation of prisons is nonetheless wrong” answers the economic case. Both lines of reasoning lead back to it.',
     why={'A': 'Background to the first reason (and “duties” is added).',
          'C': 'A reason for the last sentence.',
          'D': 'Evidence for why special measures are needed.',
          'E': 'An intermediate conclusion: one reason why privatisation is wrong.'},
     gap='“X may be true, but Y is nonetheless wrong”: Y is the conclusion.'),

dict(n=20, orig=39, answer='D', type='Weaken',
     passage=[('ctx', "Compare and contrast the initial responses of two 'major world powers' to the Haitian earthquake disaster."),
              ('evid', "Within hours, the USA had sent in hospital and assault ships and an aircraft carrier with 19 helicopters, the 82nd Airborne division with 3,500 troops and hundreds of medical personnel. It put the country's small airport back on an operational footing."),
              ('evid', "Meanwhile, across the Atlantic, the European Union (EU) geared itself up with a Brussels press conference led by its High Representative. A small group of bored-looking journalists in the Commission's lavishly appointed press room heard her stumbling through a prepared statement, in which she said that she had conveyed her 'condolences' to the UN Secretary-General, and pledged three million Euros in aid."),
              ('conc', "The USA's willingness to act proves that it is the only genuine superpower.")],
     stem='Which one of the following, if true, would most weaken the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'Haiti is much closer to the USA than to the European Union.',
              'B': 'The European Union has as many men in its armed forces as the USA.',
              'C': 'Of the countries of the EU, only Great Britain and France possess the naval and airborne capacity for major overseas operations.',
              'D': 'The countries of the EU respond to this and all humanitarian crises individually because their armed forces are allied but not integrated.',
              'E': 'The response of the USA to the crisis was criticised by France as having a hidden agenda of wanting to increase its influence in Haiti.'},
     right='Europe’s real response comes from its separate countries, not from Brussels. So the press conference is the wrong thing to compare, and it says nothing about Europe’s willingness to act.',
     why={'A': 'Explains the speed, not why Brussels offered only words and three million Euros.',
          'B': 'Same-sized forces but no action makes Europe look <b>less</b> willing: this strengthens.',
          'C': 'Shows most EU countries cannot act on this scale, which fits “only genuine superpower”.',
          'E': 'Attacks the US motive, not whether it acted or how powerful it is.'},
     gap='Check whether the comparison is fair. The EU press office is not Europe’s whole response.'),

dict(n=21, orig=40, answer='D', type='Assumption',
     passage=[('prem', 'One of the most catastrophic scenarios that could hit this planet would be a world without fish.'),
              ('conc', 'Surely this is a matter for the UN to act on – and swiftly.'),
              ('ic', 'Areas of ocean should be set aside as no-go zones for trawlers of any nationality, to enable our dwindling fish stocks to have somewhere to reproduce with impunity.'),
              ('counter', 'We are told that farmed fish is just as good,'),
              ('prem', 'but this is futile if the wild fish that are fed to the farmed fish disappear.'),
              ('conc', 'This crisis should be moved right to the top of the global agenda.')],
     stem='Which one of the following is an underlying assumption of the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'At present fish cannot reproduce safely in the wild.',
              'B': 'There is not enough publicity about the fishing crisis.',
              'C': 'Farmed fish does not taste as good as wild fish.',
              'D': 'Fish farming is impossible without wild fish as food.',
              'E': 'Foreign trawlers are responsible for dwindling fish stocks.'},
     right='Farming is called “futile” if wild fish disappear. That only follows if farmed fish cannot be fed on anything else.',
     why={'A': 'Too strong. Stocks are dwindling, but the argument does not need fish to be unable to breed at all.',
          'B': 'Getting onto the global agenda is not the same as publicity.',
          'C': 'Taste is never part of the reasoning.',
          'E': 'Trawlers of “any nationality” are banned, so foreign ones are not singled out.'},
     gap='The answer to the opposing view (“farmed fish is just as good”) is where the hidden assumption sits.'),

dict(n=22, orig=41, answer='C', type='Strengthen',
     passage=[('ctx', 'Many rich countries protect their farming sectors with generous subsidies.'),
              ('counter', 'It may be claimed that this policy can benefit countries in which people are starving or malnourished by encouraging farmers to produce more food than can be consumed in their own countries.'),
              ('ic', 'But the policy is not in the best interests of poorer countries.'),
              ('prem', 'Poor countries do not want charitable handouts – they want to develop the ability to feed their populations by their own efforts. If the amount spent on the European Commission’s Common Agricultural Policy were invested in, say, irrigation schemes, that would transform the agriculture of many impoverished nations.'),
              ('conc', 'This would surely be a better way to solve the problem of world hunger.')],
     stem='Which one of the following, if true, would most strengthen the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'Malnutrition is less likely to occur in countries with slow population growth.',
              'B': 'Wars and natural disasters such as floods are major causes of malnutrition.',
              'C': 'Much of the excess food produced by rich countries rots before it can be exported.',
              'D': 'Without subsidies to farmers in rich countries, the countryside would deteriorate.',
              'E': 'Agriculture in poor countries is less mechanised than in rich countries.'},
     right='If the surplus rots before export, subsidies do little for the hungry. That knocks out the opposing claim, so investing the money instead looks better.',
     why={'A': 'Population growth plays no part in the argument.',
          'B': 'Irrigation would not stop wars or floods: this weakens.',
          'D': 'A reason to keep the subsidies: this weakens.',
          'E': 'Points to machinery, not irrigation, and says nothing about subsidies failing.'},
     gap='A “better way” argument is strengthened by showing the current way does not work.'),

